<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lebedos</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Lebedos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Ionian city between Teos and Colophon, one of the twelve of the Ionian League, later home of the travelling Dionysiac actors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lebedos was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a>, traditionally under Andraemon, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codrus">Codrus</a>, on the coast between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/teos">Teos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a> c. 1000 BCE. Though small, it was one of the twelve members of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government. It lived from farming and fishing and had hot springs nearby. Lebedos fell to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Lydia</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, and paid tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> as a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. It revolted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 412 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> moved many of its inhabitants to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus</a> c. 290 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemies</a> refounded it as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barca">Ptolemais</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of Dionysiac artists later settled there. Lebedos passed into Roman Asia after 133 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> described it as nearly deserted.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lebedos.jpg" alt="Lebedos" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: Ms. Sevil Ege · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kisik_or_Lebedos_Peninsula_Urkmez_Seferihisar_Izmir_Turkey.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lebedos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lebedus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=lebedos" aria-label="Lebedos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5352 809 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lebedos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5672 1065L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5672 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5672 1065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=lebedos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

